# rules: csharp-sql-built-from-request
# languages: c_sharp
# fixture: csharp/CWE-89.cs
# from: CWE-89 (SQL Injection)
#
# The command text is assembled from something the caller sent, so the caller
# writes SQL. `'; DROP TABLE Orders --` in a query string is the whole attack
# and it has not needed anything cleverer for twenty years.
#
# The two sources are the two ways ASP.NET hands a request to a method, and
# both are needed. `Request.Query["name"]` is the old spelling and is caught by
# reading the right-hand side of a declaration, because `Request.Query[$K]`
# cannot be written as a pattern on its own - the C# grammar reads it standing
# alone as an array type, `Request.Query[]`, and says so. The modern spelling
# is a parameter: MVC binds `Get(string city)` from the query string, the route
# or the body, so the parameter *is* the request and there is no accessor
# anywhere in the method to find. A rule that knew only the first source missed
# every controller written since 2016.
#
# The sinks are the three ways a raw string becomes SQL: the command object,
# Entity Framework's two escape hatches, and an assignment to CommandText.
# `FromSqlRaw` and `ExecuteSqlRaw` are named for what they do, and their safe
# siblings - `FromSqlInterpolated`, `ExecuteSqlInterpolated` - are deliberately
# not here: they take a FormattableString and parameterise the holes, so the
# interpolation that looks identical in the source is the safe one. Reporting
# those would teach people the distinction is noise.
#
# What is not a sanitizer is worth saying too. `Parameters.AddWithValue` sits
# in the fixture beside a parameterised query and the rule stays quiet there,
# but not because the call was seen - because nothing untrusted reached the
# command text in the first place. A rule that treated the call as a sanitizer
# would go quiet on a method that parameterises one hole and concatenates the
# next, which is the shape most of these bugs actually have.
#
# Every argument hole is written beside a `$$$_`, and in C# it has to be. A
# call pattern with a single hole in argument position compiles to two
# readings, and the second binds the hole to the whole argument list whatever
# its length - so `new SqlCommand($Q)` reports `("...", conn)` on a two
# argument call, at the wrong column and for the wrong reason. See the guide.
#
# The parameter source is confined to files that are actually MVC. This is the
# lesson the corpus taught and it was expensive: `public $T $M($$$_, string $P,
# $$$_) { $$$_ }` says "a public method taking a string", which in a controller
# means the request and in a utility class means nothing at all. Run over
# ShareX - a desktop application - the path rule reported twenty-seven
# findings, every one of them a `string filePath` parameter of a file helper.
# A file that imports ASP.NET MVC, or declares a class deriving from something
# called Controller, is the test; a file that does neither has no request in it
# and the parameter half of this rule is switched off there.

["$T $V = $S;"] as $assigned
| ["public $T $M($$$_, string $P, $$$_) { $$$_ }",
   "public $T $M(string $P) { $$$_ }"] as $bound
| ["using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;", "using System.Web.Mvc;",
   "using System.Web.Http;", "using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components;"] as $mvcImports
| ["class $C : $B { $$$_ }"] as $classes
| ["new SqlCommand($Q, $$$_)",
   "new NpgsqlCommand($Q, $$$_)", "new MySqlCommand($Q, $$$_)",
   "new OleDbCommand($Q, $$$_)", "new OdbcCommand($Q, $$$_)",
   "$D.FromSqlRaw($Q, $$$_);", "$T $V = $D.FromSqlRaw($Q, $$$_);",
   "$D.ExecuteSqlRaw($Q, $$$_);", "$T $V = $D.ExecuteSqlRaw($Q, $$$_);",
   "$C.CommandText = $Q;"] as $sinks
| "Request\\s*\\.\\s*(Query|Form|Headers|Cookies|QueryString|Params|RouteValues)\\b" as $fromRequest
| scan_ast("*.cs"; $assigned + $bound + $mvcImports + $classes + $sinks) as $all
| ( ($all | of($assigned) | where_capture("S"; $fromRequest) | focus("S"))
    + ($all | of($bound) | focus("P")
       | in_files_with( ($all | of($mvcImports))
                        + ($all | of($classes) | where_capture("B"; "Controller")) )) ) as $untrusted
| ($all | of($sinks) | focus("Q"))
| reaching($untrusted; [])
| finding("csharp-sql-built-from-request";
    "this command text is assembled from the request, so the caller decides "
    + "what SQL runs rather than what it runs against")
| report
